{"causes": ["The assassination of King Salomon of Brittany in 874 CE created a power vacuum and ignited a succession crisis among the Breton nobility.", "Pascweten's inheritance of significant landed estates and control over valuable saltworks provided him with the economic resources necessary to pursue royal claims.", "The established prominence of the Ridoredh family of Vannes afforded Pascweten a strong aristocratic lineage and regional influence within southeastern Brittany."], "effects": ["Pascweten's prolonged rivalry with Gurvand led to a destructive civil war that severely weakened the Kingdom of Brittany.", "His alliances with Frankish magnates during the succession crisis increased external Frankish influence within Breton affairs.", "The instability caused by Pascweten's conflict with Gurvand paved the way for the eventual rise of Alan the Great, who would temporarily restore unity to Brittany."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "pascweten", "sourceName": "Pascweten", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "succession-crisis-of-brittany-874-ce", "targetName": "Succession Crisis of Brittany (874 CE)", "context": "Pascweten was a principal claimant to the throne during the brutal succession crisis that followed King Salomon's death in 874 CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "salomon-king-of-brittany", "sourceName": "Salomon, King of Brittany", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "pascweten", "targetName": "Pascweten", "context": "The assassination of King Salomon in 874 CE directly precipitated Pascweten's emergence as a major contender for the Breton throne."}, {"sourceSlug": "pascweten", "sourceName": "Pascweten", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "vannes-france", "targetName": "Vannes, France", "context": "Pascweten served as the powerful Count of Vannes, which served as his primary seat of power and economic base."}, {"sourceSlug": "pascweten", "sourceName": "Pascweten", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "gurvand", "targetName": "Gurvand", "context": "Pascweten initially collaborated with Gurvand in the assassination of King Salomon, but their alliance quickly dissolved into a bitter rivalry for the throne."}, {"sourceSlug": "pascweten", "sourceName": "Pascweten", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "breton-civil-war-874-877", "targetName": "Breton Civil War (874-877)", "context": "Pascweten's relentless struggle against Gurvand was a primary driver of the Breton Civil War from 874 to 877 CE, causing widespread devastation."}], "places": [{"name": "Vannes, France", "role": "County Seat"}, {"name": "Redon, France", "role": "Patronage Site"}, {"name": "Brittany, France", "role": "Contested Territory"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Pascweten", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "pascweten"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2526916", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pascweten"], "tags": ["Monarchs & Rulers", "political-systems", "empire-and-colonialism", "geopolitical-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Pascweten's ambition and conflict with Gurvand directly triggered a devastating civil war in Brittany from 874-877 CE, weakening the kingdom against external threats. His actions contributed to a period of instability that profoundly shaped the subsequent political landscape of the region.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-10-02T08:24:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36983584944", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Pascweten was the count of Vannes and a claimant to the rule of Brittany. He was a son of Ridoredh of Vannes, a prominent and wealthy aristocrat first associated with the court of Erispoe in the 850s. He owned vast landed estates and salt works in southeastern Brittany and was a patron of Redon Abbey. Pascweten was a monarchs & rulers from Armorica (850 CE, Armorica–877 CE). Recognized as a landmark figure with global significance (referenced in 0 Wikipedia editions). Relevant to political systems and imperial and colonial studies.", "newValue": "Pascweten, Count of Vannes, emerged as a formidable claimant to the Kingdom of Brittany following the assassination of King Salomon in 874 CE, plunging the region into a period of intense civil strife. A scion of the powerful Ridoredh family of Vannes, he inherited vast estates, including lucrative saltworks in southeastern Brittany, and cultivated strong ties with ecclesiastical institutions like Redon Abbey, which benefited from his patronage. His ambition and significant wealth positioned him as a central figure in the complex political landscape of late 9th-century Armorica, where Frankish pressures and Viking raids further destabilized Breton autonomy.

His bid for the Breton throne was characterized by a bitter and protracted rivalry with Gurvand, Count of Rennes. This internecine conflict, which began immediately after Salomon's death, saw the two magnates engage in a series of military confrontations and political maneuvers, effectively dividing Brittany. Pascweten often allied with Frankish lords, such as the Count of Anjou, to bolster his position against Gurvand, a strategy that underscored the precarious balance of power in the region. Despite his military prowess and resource base, he never fully secured the kingship, dying in 877 CE amidst the ongoing power struggle, likely from an epidemic that also claimed Gurvand.

Pascweten's relentless pursuit of power significantly contributed to the fragmentation of Breton authority, weakening the kingdom's ability to resist external threats. His death, followed closely by Gurvand's, left a power vacuum that was eventually filled by Alan the Great, who would temporarily reunite Brittany. The legacy of Pascweten's ambition, however, served as a stark example of the centrifugal forces within the Breton nobility, demonstrating how localized power bases could undermine the fragile unity forged by earlier Breton kings like Nominoë and Erispoë."}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-02T08:24:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36983584944", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "850 CE, Armorica"}, {"title": "Dynastic succession and sovereign authority traditions", "type": "Idea", "year": "850 CE, Armorica"}], "newValue": ["The assassination of King Salomon of Brittany in 874 CE created a power vacuum and ignited a succession crisis among the Breton nobility.", "Pascweten's inheritance of significant landed estates and control over valuable saltworks provided him with the economic resources necessary to pursue royal claims.", "The established prominence of the Ridoredh family of Vannes afforded Pascweten a strong aristocratic lineage and regional influence within southeastern Brittany."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-10-02T08:24:09Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#36983584944", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Established dynastic legacies and territorial boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "877 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "877 CE"}], "newValue": ["Pascweten's prolonged rivalry with Gurvand led to a destructive civil war that severely weakened the Kingdom of Brittany.",